n  israeli bus stop israeli soldiers  guarding it that's for the settlers to  be able to move from one settlement  within the west bank to another this is  just the sort of place  where incidents like this tend to happen  today there are more than three quarters  of a million israeli jewish settlers  living in the west bank  that's more than triple the number who  were here in 1993  when the oslo accords promised a piece  which never came  among them is raquel gordon from london  originally  when we moved here about 20 years ago  that arab town was very very small  and it's grown exponentially since we  moved it but do you not see a sort of  that you're being a bit hypocritical in  the sense that you're saying that the  arab town has grown  but but your town wasn't here at all  this is  as far as i'm concerned it's israeli  territory  and um the whole of  the whole of israel belongs to the  jewish people  for decades the palestinian quest has  been their own state  with east jerusalem as their capital and  with jewish settlers moving back out of  the west bank  but is that still achievable or should  israel's deal with gulf arabs prompt a  new palestinian strategy  i think what we have to do as  palestinians is recognize that there is  an apartheid one-state reality  except that as a starting point but not  accept it as our future  it really stresses the need for  recalibration of the palestinian  struggle  that we can't rely on people that we  thought were allies  that we have to sort of seek out  allyship friendship  um interconnectedness with with other  struggles with other peoples around the  world  there are hints of that already  parallels drawn between perceived  injustices here and elsewhere  palestinian lives matter is the slogan  our journey ends in ramallah a statue of  nelson mandela stands as a symbol  of equality and freedom it's an irony  that the young palestinian rapper at his  feet  told us he didn't know who the old man  was  you don't know him e